WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.802
-Our first guest tonight is
a best-selling author,

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:05.905
the Emmy-winning host of MSNBC's
"All In With Chris Hayes,"

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:07.740
and the host
of the weekly podcast

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.042
"Why Is This Happening?"
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:12.045
our friend Chris Hayes,
everybody.

00:00:12.045 --> 00:00:15.148
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:20.653
♪♪

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:26.325
♪♪

00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:28.728
Welcome back, my friend.
-We're so close now.

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:30.730
The last time I was here,
I was over there

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.298
so COVID couldn't get you.

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:34.567
-Before we had an audience,
we went over there,

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:37.170
and we had very firm words
with COVID ahead of time.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.505
We were like,
"Don't get between us."

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:39.906
-"Don't you dare."
-"Don't you dare."

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:43.342
We talked about this recently,
not on-camera,

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.311
but we just had the eighth
anniversary of our show.

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:47.213
You just had the ninth
anniversary of your show.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:51.217
And it does feel like it's been
a hundred years, right?

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:52.651
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:00:52.651 --> 00:00:54.721
I mean, I think -- I think

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:58.791
Trump into plague into war
has been,

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:01.427
like, a really intense run,
I would say.

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:03.630
-Right, because I do think
there's this --

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.032
you know,
at every turn,

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:08.234
when maybe people have framed it

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.303
as "We're finally
going back to normal,"

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:11.838
something incredibly abnormal

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:13.940
and with potentially
even higher stakes happens.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.209
-Yeah, and I think, like --
I can't tell --

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:17.743
I think about this a lot.
I can't tell if it's

00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:19.212
just that I'm aging,

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:21.981
and the longer your life goes,
the more opportunity you have

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:24.650
to watch once-in-a-century
things happen.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:25.918
-Yeah.
-Or we've been through

00:01:25.918 --> 00:01:27.854
a particularly insane period.

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:30.790
But I remember, like,
when I was younger,

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.758
I was, like, a playwright
or I'd watch TV shows

00:01:32.758 --> 00:01:34.260
and there would be
too much stuff happening

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.428
and some, you know,
tragedy from above,

00:01:36.428 --> 00:01:37.831
and I'd be like,
"Aw, that's not realistic."

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:39.031
And now everything I'm watching,

00:01:39.031 --> 00:01:41.033
I'm like, "Yeah,
that totally could happen.

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:44.370
Like, there's no plot twist,
there's no, like, you know,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.439
deus ex machina tragedy
I'm not like,

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:47.673
"Yeah, that makes sense."
-That's true.

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:49.108
-That would definitely happen.
-You're right.

00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:51.978
We're basically living
in a Shonda Rhimes show.

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:54.347
-Yes!
Just one thing after another.

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:56.482
-Where you're like, "How did
they come up with that?"

00:01:56.482 --> 00:01:58.851
-I mean, I even had the, like --
I mean, the timing --

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:00.653
and again, like, you know,
the war,

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:03.189
the principal victims of the war
are the people of Ukraine,

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:06.325
and that will then cascade out
through the rest of the world

00:02:06.325 --> 00:02:08.093
in brutal, brutal ways.

00:02:08.093 --> 00:02:09.896
But I do remember
having a thought of, like,

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:12.598
"Alright, well,
this omicron wave --

00:02:12.598 --> 00:02:16.001
get over that wave,
and then, you know, maybe,

00:02:16.001 --> 00:02:17.470
just maybe [Knocks desk]

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.971
things maybe chill out
a little bit."

00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:21.240
It's like, absolutely not.
-Absolutely not,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.243
and the other thing,
you've covered conflicts before,

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:26.545
but this does seem
to have moved faster

00:02:26.545 --> 00:02:30.116
than anything
that we've certainly dealt with

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:32.151
in the times
that we've been on television.

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:33.519
-It has moved faster.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:36.055
There's a real waking-nightmare
quality to it,

00:02:36.055 --> 00:02:39.959
partly because it's so terrible
for everyone

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:42.695
and no one wants it
but one person.

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:44.797
-Yeah.
-And you're just watching this.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:45.998
We were watching it
in the run-up,

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:47.400
being like, "Don't do this,
do not do this,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.601
don't do this.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.036
This will be awful
for everyone."

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:51.704
And you know, it's bad for --

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:53.005
obviously, it's bad
for Ukrainians.

00:02:53.005 --> 00:02:56.109
It's going to be bad
for Egyptian bread buyers.

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:58.611
It's going to be bad
for Russian oligarchs

00:02:58.611 --> 00:02:59.846
whose yachts are seized.
It's going to be bad

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:01.147
for average Russians
who are going to have

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:03.616
their pensions devalued
by 50% to 80%.

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.551
It's going to be bad for NATO
and the West

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:08.721
as they see gas prices going --
It's bad, bad, bad in every way,

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:10.156
and you're just watching
this entire thing,

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:11.724
thinking, like, "Don't do it."

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:14.293
And this one person is
the only one that wants it,

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:16.129
and that's the person
that matters right now.

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:20.533
-It is shocking to think
that one person has that --

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:23.335
their hand on a lever
that sort of contains

00:03:23.335 --> 00:03:25.872
that much power behind it.
-It's shocking and terrifying.

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:28.274
-One of the things that is
different about this,

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:32.011
and none of us think that you
should just blindly touch --

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:34.547
sorry -- blindly trust
the intelligence agencies.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:37.115
But, you know, obviously,
it's been a bad history,

00:03:37.115 --> 00:03:40.619
in recent years, of the
American intelligence agency

00:03:40.619 --> 00:03:42.388
getting things right, but
they really got this right.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:44.624
And it seems like Biden
took this information

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:48.561
and he shared it with our allies
and, almost to the week,

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:51.163
got it right
that Putin was going to

00:03:51.163 --> 00:03:53.899
actually go through with this.
-I described it

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:56.135
as the Iraq War in reverse,
in terms of

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:58.238
the public presentation
of American intelligence.

00:03:58.238 --> 00:03:59.973
I mean, you even had
Antony Blinken,

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:05.211
who was sitting at the same seat
in the U.S. -- in the U.N. --

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:07.480
where Colin Powell
gave his famous speech,

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.915
explicitly referencing it,
saying, "I'm not here

00:04:09.915 --> 00:04:11.550
to start a war;
I'm here to stop one."

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.919
They took a very high-leverage
strategy, which was to be

00:04:13.919 --> 00:04:16.122
very granular
about the intelligence.

00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:17.690
And a lot of the world didn't
want to believe it

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.158
because a lot of the world
thought this was a bad idea

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.193
and didn't want it to happen.
When it did happen

00:04:21.193 --> 00:04:23.729
almost precisely the way
the U.S. intelligence apparatus

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:25.832
and the Biden administration
said,

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:28.134
I do think it produced
a lot of credibility

00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:29.535
they have now leveraged

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.370
to sort of
get this coalition together.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.372
-It is both --
Again, it goes back

00:04:33.372 --> 00:04:35.975
to what you were saying.
I think the idea

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:37.310
that there's this coalition is

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.345
something
we can all be happy about.

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:41.981
And it does seem like
a strong -- again, early days --

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:44.150
strong coalition.
But it doesn't really matter

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.519
when you talk about isolating
someone like Vladimir Putin.

00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:48.654
It seems like he's perfectly
fine, living in isolation.

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:51.824
-I mean, the question now is --
and then what question,

00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:53.525
what possible off-ramp
there can be,

00:04:53.525 --> 00:04:57.997
what possible level of pain
for the Russian government,

00:04:57.997 --> 00:04:59.332
for Vladimir Putin personally,

00:04:59.332 --> 00:05:01.000
for the Russian people,
for oligarchs.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.402
What would produce a decision

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:05.371
that this was
not worth the price?

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:07.407
And right now -- I mean,
he's been very explicit.

00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:09.675
He said the other day
something like --

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:12.645
the effect of, "Nothing
will ever make me back off

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:15.214
from the notion
that these are one people."

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:16.716
-Right.
-It does not sound

00:05:16.716 --> 00:05:19.852
like a person who is ready
to get on an off-ramp.

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:22.855
-A lot of arguments are made
that certainly --

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:24.690
I don't think they're
good-faith arguments,

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.092
but people say about me,

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:27.559
"Why are you so obsessed
with Donald Trump?

00:05:27.559 --> 00:05:28.661
Why are you still
talking about him?"

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:29.996
I imagine you get
the same sort of thing.

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:31.463
-We do.
-He is deeply connected

00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:33.832
to not just the news today,
but I also think the future.

00:05:33.832 --> 00:05:35.934
Right now,
if I had money to bet --

00:05:35.934 --> 00:05:37.937
I don't 'cause
it's all in rubles.

00:05:37.937 --> 00:05:39.705
I blew it.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:39.705 --> 00:05:40.973
I don't want to talk about it.
I had an investor.

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:42.675
-I told you
not to make that trade.

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:45.144
You texted me.
-I'm already regretting it.

00:05:45.144 --> 00:05:49.882
But, you know, I believe
he will, in all likelihood,

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:51.317
be the Republican nominee.

00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:52.718
-Yeah, that's the problem,
right?

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:55.454
I mean, if he's not the most
likely nominee for President

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:58.423
in 2024,
and an even-odds chance

00:05:58.423 --> 00:06:00.659
of being the president again,
then, yeah, screw him,

00:06:00.659 --> 00:06:01.894
forget about him.

00:06:01.894 --> 00:06:04.063
But he is the leader
of the Republican Party.

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:09.101
He was a once-in-history --
He led, essentially,

00:06:09.101 --> 00:06:12.371
a once-in-history attempt
at American democracy.

00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:13.606
Never really happened before.

00:06:13.606 --> 00:06:15.975
I mean, Fort Sumter, you know,
the secession.

00:06:15.975 --> 00:06:20.480
So he is the most acute threat
to American democracy around,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.882
and probably
in the nation's recent memory

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:24.717
and perhaps since the Civil War.

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:26.218
And he's still in power.

00:06:26.218 --> 00:06:29.121
So he's still --
I mean, the question

00:06:29.121 --> 00:06:31.256
about the perseverance
of American democracy

00:06:31.256 --> 00:06:34.460
is one of the major stories
of our time,

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.796
if not the major story.
And he is the --

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:39.499
You know, he is the villain
in that story.

00:06:39.499 --> 00:06:41.534
You can't not talk about it.

00:06:41.534 --> 00:06:44.970
-We were also talking backstage
how deeply disappointing it is,

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.206
in a time where anyone would
love to have a distraction,

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:49.274
that Major League Baseball --
we're both baseball fans --

00:06:49.274 --> 00:06:52.345
is currently in a lockout.
You grew up in New York,

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:53.880
but your dad raised you
a Cubs fan.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.381
-Yep.
-Because he was from Chicago.

00:06:55.381 --> 00:06:56.782
You threw out a first pitch.

00:06:56.782 --> 00:06:57.983
How did your first pitch
at Wrigley go?

00:06:57.983 --> 00:06:59.485
-It went very well.
-Really?

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:00.753
-Yes.
-Alright.

00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:02.855
I said that in a way
that I'm already regretting.

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:04.556
[ Laughter ]

00:07:04.556 --> 00:07:06.325
Now, slow down, here.

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:08.995
-Well, let me say,
it was not a strike,

00:07:08.995 --> 00:07:13.032
but it was not --
it was a ball near the zone.

00:07:13.032 --> 00:07:14.300
-Gotcha.
-Okay?

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.335
-Like, the umpire would have
had to make a call?

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:18.637
-Yeah, right.
I wasn't bouncing it up there.

00:07:18.637 --> 00:07:20.205
-Yeah.
-I got advice --

00:07:20.205 --> 00:07:21.641
I ran into Bill Murray
in a restaurant,

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:23.909
the summer before
I threw out the first pitch.

00:07:23.909 --> 00:07:25.444
-Fantastic.
-Amazing.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:27.380
Definitely struck up
a little conversation.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:28.548
He had thrown out a first pitch.

00:07:28.548 --> 00:07:30.182
And the last words he said to me
were, "Aim high."

00:07:30.182 --> 00:07:32.652
'Cause you don't want to
bounce that thing.

00:07:32.652 --> 00:07:34.252
You'd rather sail it
than bounce it.

00:07:34.252 --> 00:07:36.455
So I aimed high, and,
yeah, it was super fun.

00:07:36.455 --> 00:07:37.823
Have you done the first pitch?

00:07:37.823 --> 00:07:39.625
-I did the first pitch
at Fenway Park.

00:07:39.625 --> 00:07:40.859
-Oh.
-Yeah.

00:07:40.859 --> 00:07:43.796
I wish I'd run into Bill Murray.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:43.796 --> 00:07:45.231
-Wait, did you, like,
50 cent it?

00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:48.834
-No, it was perfectly fine.
I had Billy Miller,

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:50.636
who played for both
the Cubs and the Red Sox --

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:52.070
he actually did me a solid.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:53.439
He went out and caught it.

00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:55.908
So I actually had an active
player on the roster,

00:07:55.908 --> 00:07:58.443
catching it.
And he did me a real solid.

00:07:58.443 --> 00:08:00.446
He kind of had to do this.

00:08:00.446 --> 00:08:01.947
He went and got it
before it bounced.

00:08:01.947 --> 00:08:03.783
-Okay.
-But it required --

00:08:03.783 --> 00:08:05.951
-And then he framed it up?
-Oh, he brought it up.

00:08:05.951 --> 00:08:08.720
The other thing people --
they should also tell you is,

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.324
like, almost no one
is paying attention.

00:08:11.324 --> 00:08:13.259
Like, when you think
about every baseball game

00:08:13.259 --> 00:08:15.027
you've gone to in your life,
they do it

00:08:15.027 --> 00:08:16.862
like an hour and a half
before the first pitch.

00:08:16.862 --> 00:08:18.430
-Correct.
-No one is there.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:20.299
-Right.
-There's always like two guys

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:22.200
who are just there
to make you feel bad.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:23.536
-Yeah.
-Yeah, they, like,

00:08:23.536 --> 00:08:25.337
show up early
just to, like, heckle.

00:08:25.337 --> 00:08:28.740
-You basically just want to stay
off a YouTube bloopers reel.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.376
-That's it.
-That's it.

00:08:30.376 --> 00:08:33.645
You don't want to be
on the 50 cent compilation.

00:08:33.645 --> 00:08:36.182
-Right, that's true.
There's no YouTube compilation

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:38.084
of, like, "The Best
Celebrity First Pitches."

00:08:38.084 --> 00:08:40.118
-None.
-They're like, "Chris Hayes!"

00:08:40.118 --> 00:08:42.187
Wham!
[ Laughter ]

00:08:42.187 --> 00:08:43.923
-Look at that breaking slider.

00:08:43.923 --> 00:08:45.391
-I was lucky enough to do --

00:08:45.391 --> 00:08:47.526
You have a great podcast,
"Why Is This Happening?"

00:08:47.526 --> 00:08:49.695
You're doing a special series
that I was lucky enough

00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:52.097
to be a guest on, a four-parter.
-Yeah, it's super fun.

00:08:52.097 --> 00:08:54.299
It's called "The Future Of."
And again, I found myself --

00:08:54.299 --> 00:08:55.801
and maybe other people
feel this way --

00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:59.204
it is very hard to conceive of
futures that are not dystopian.

00:08:59.204 --> 00:09:00.739
-Yeah.
-You know, like, you go back

00:09:00.739 --> 00:09:02.441
and you look at the
Columbian Exposition

00:09:02.441 --> 00:09:04.343
in the 1890s
or, you know, "Metropolis"

00:09:04.343 --> 00:09:06.112
or even "Star Trek" -- right? --
these visions of

00:09:06.112 --> 00:09:07.813
like, "We're going to space," or

00:09:07.813 --> 00:09:08.947
"We're solving
humanity's problems."

00:09:08.947 --> 00:09:10.782
And "The Jetsons," even,
at a cartoon level.

00:09:10.782 --> 00:09:12.918
Like, there's no equivalent
to that now.

00:09:12.918 --> 00:09:14.853
All we think about the future is
like "Blade Runner"

00:09:14.853 --> 00:09:16.488
or, you know, "The Road."

00:09:16.488 --> 00:09:18.957
-I'd give anything for
"Blade Runner."

00:09:18.957 --> 00:09:20.092
-That would be
a huge improvement.

00:09:20.092 --> 00:09:21.593
-Oh, my God, if we could
get "Blade Runner"?

00:09:21.593 --> 00:09:23.729
If my kids could grow up
in a "Blade Runner" world,

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:25.430
based on how things are going,
I'd be so happy.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.433
-I guess that's a best-case
scenario at this point.

00:09:28.433 --> 00:09:30.368
So, yeah, we're just
doing this series

00:09:30.368 --> 00:09:32.571
and sort of looking at
the future of different things

00:09:32.571 --> 00:09:34.273
and thinking about how --
I think particularly

00:09:34.273 --> 00:09:36.442
because we're coming out of
this two-year period

00:09:36.442 --> 00:09:38.844
in which everything changed,
I'm really obsessed

00:09:38.844 --> 00:09:40.646
with, like, what stays
and what goes.

00:09:40.646 --> 00:09:42.648
You know, your shirts,
for instance --

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:44.116
those are staying.
-They're staying for now.

00:09:44.116 --> 00:09:45.584
-We're carrying that forward.

00:09:45.584 --> 00:09:48.587
You know, the audience is back.

00:09:48.587 --> 00:09:50.456
-The audience is back.
-We like having them back.

00:09:50.456 --> 00:09:52.324
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:52.324 --> 00:09:53.859
-So there's some good.
-Pandering.

00:09:53.859 --> 00:09:56.728
Pandering also.
-Yeah, pandering is back.

00:09:56.728 --> 00:10:00.232
-Pandering is back.
So, yeah, we've got

00:10:00.232 --> 00:10:02.100
some cool folks
talking about everything

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.103
from the future of energy
to the future of friendship

00:10:04.103 --> 00:10:05.971
to the future of entertainment
with you.

00:10:05.971 --> 00:10:07.339
-Alright, well, hey,
thank you so much

00:10:07.339 --> 00:10:08.974
for being here.
You guys, that's Chris Hayes.

00:10:08.974 --> 00:10:10.876
-Great to be here.
